      "completion_tokens": 1115,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. The Three States\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking and washing.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients in the blood and for dissolving waste in the body.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body from overheating.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, water is less dense as a solid (ice) than as a liquid. This is why ice floats on water, which insulates lakes and oceans, allowing life to survive underneath the ice during winter.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life. It is the primary component of all living things (humans are about 60% water). It is used in cells to:\n*   Transport nutrients and oxygen.\n*   Remove waste.\n*   Regulate body temperature.\n*   Lubricate joints and organs.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule that is chemically complex enough to make life on Earth possible."
